The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
control_cpu = 0;
if (!cpu_online(control_cpu)) {
    control_cpu = get_cpu();
    put_cpu();
}
```
Reason:
The use of `smp_processor_id()` was replaced with `get_cpu()/put_cpu()`, which still relied on the currently running CPU. This could lead to confusion and potential crashes if the CPU was not online, as it did not guarantee that the control CPU was always an available online CPU.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
control_cpu = cpumask_first(cpu_online_mask);
```
Reason:
This change directly assigns the control CPU to the first online CPU, ensuring that it is always valid and available, simplifying the logic and avoiding the issues associated with using the currently running CPU.

